78}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Auschwitz","confidence":0.99},{"name":"Hungary","confidence":0.92},{"name":"Mauthausen","confidence":0.82}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Healing from trauma becomes possible when people confront painful experiences, forgive captors or perpetrators, and reclaim freedom from the prisons of their own minds.","confidence":0.94},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.92},"profileConfidence":0.96,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"c085797cac3129aae36274a0519597cb350e8efcfe55979a278ed2c5ca474f3c","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["author_included_for_review: Edith Eva Eger","central_places_hit_schema_cap"]},"summary":"\"A powerful, moving memoir--and a practical guide to healing--written by Dr. Edith Eva Eger, an eminent psychologist whose own experiences as a Holocaust survivor help her treat patients and allow them to escape the prisons of their own minds. Edith Eger was sixteen years old when the Nazis came to her hometown in Hungary and took her Jewish family to an internment center and then to Auschwitz. Her parents were sent to the gas chamber by Joseph Mengele soon after they arrived at the camp. Hours later Mengele demanded that Edie dance a waltz to 'The Blue Danube' and rewarded her with a loaf of bread that she shared with her fellow prisoners. These women later helped save Edie's life. Edie and her sister survived Auschwitz, were transferred to the Mauthausen and Gunskirchen camps in Austria, and managed to live until the American troops liberated the camps in 1945 and found Edie in a pile of dying bodies. One of the few living Holocaust survivors to remember the horrors of the camps, Edie has chosen to forgive her captors and find joy in her life every day. Years after she was liberated from the concentration camps Edie went back to college to study psychology. She combines her clinical knowledge and her own experiences with trauma to help others who have experienced painful events large and small. Dr.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychologists","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Biography","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychological aspects","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Personal narratives","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Holocaust, Jewish (1939-1945)","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Concentration camp inmates","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Holocaust survivors","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Suffering","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychic trauma","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Post-traumatic stress disorder","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Holocaust, jewish (1939-1945), personal narratives","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychologists, biography","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-choice-edith-eger"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"New York Times reviewed","sourceId":"so